## Why This Is Asked

Duplicate values are common in real-world data sets. Traditional 2-way quicksort performs poorly with many duplicates, leading to unnecessary comparisons and degraded performance. 3-way partitioning addresses this by grouping equal elements together, significantly reducing the algorithm's work.

## Key Concepts

- **3-way partitioning (Dutch national flag algorithm)**: Separates array into < pivot, == pivot, and > pivot regions
- **Randomized pivot selection**: Prevents worst-case O(n²) scenarios on already sorted or adversarial input
- **In-place sorting**: Operates with O(1) additional space, using only O(log n) expected stack depth
- **Performance optimization**: Reduces comparisons from O(n log n) to O(n) when many duplicates exist

## Code Example

```javascript
function quicksort3(a, lo = 0, hi = a.length - 1) {
  if (lo >= hi) return;

  // Random pivot selection
  const pivot = a[Math.floor(Math.random() * (hi - lo + 1)) + lo];
  let lt = lo, i = lo, gt = hi;

  // 3-way partitioning
  while (i <= gt) {
    if (a[i] < pivot) {
      [a[lt], a[i]] = [a[i], a[lt]];
      lt++;
      i++;
    } else if (a[i] > pivot) {
      [a[i], a[gt]] = [a[gt], a[i]];
      gt--;
    } else {
      i++; // a[i] === pivot
    }
  }

  // Recursively sort subarrays
  quicksort3(a, lo, lt - 1);
  quicksort3(a, gt + 1, hi);
}
```

## Partitioning Strategy

The algorithm maintains three regions during partitioning:
- `[lo, lt-1]`: elements < pivot
- `[lt, gt]`: elements === pivot (being processed)
- `[gt+1, hi]`: elements > pivot

As we iterate, elements are swapped into their appropriate region, with equal elements naturally forming the middle section that needs no further processing.

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to model task dependencies as a DAG and compute an optimal parallel schedule using topological ordering and dynamic programming.

## Key Concepts

- **Topological sort**: Ordering nodes such that all prerequisites come before dependent tasks
- **DAG DP**: Computing the longest path in a directed acyclic graph
- **Cycle detection**: Using indegree counts to identify cycles (if not all nodes are processed)
- **Time complexity**: O(n + m) where n is nodes and m is edges

## Code Example

```javascript
function earliestFinish(n, edges, duration) {
  const adj = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);

  for (const [u, v] of edges) {
    adj[u].push(v);
    indeg[v]++;
  }

  const finish = duration.slice();
  const q = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (indeg[i] === 0) q.push(i);
  }

  let processed = 0;

  while (q.length) {
    const u = q.shift();
    processed++;

    for (const v of adj[u]) {
      finish[v] = Math.max(finish[v], finish[u] + duration[v]);
      indeg[v]--;
      if (indeg[v] === 0) q.push(v);
    }
  }

  if (processed !== n) return -1;
  return Math.max(...finish);
}
```

## Key Takeaways

- Unlimited workers means tasks run in parallel when dependencies allow
- The finish time of a task is the maximum of (prerequisite finish + its own duration)
- The pipeline completes when the slowest path (critical path) finishes

## Why This Is Asked

Tests DP design for constrained substring deletions, palindrome preprocessing, and reconstruction of selections. Combines memoized and tabulated forms, plus memory/time tradeoffs on a practical string problem.

## Key Concepts

- Palindrome preprocessing (center expansion / DP)
- DP over prefixes with up to k deletions
- Reconstructing solution under tie scenarios

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxReward(s, k){
  const n = s.length;
  const P = Array.from({length:n}, ()=>Array(n).fill(false));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){ P[i][i]=true; for(let j=i+1;j<n;j++){ /* fill later */ } }
  // assume proper palindrome fill here
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  for(let t=0;t<=k;t++) dp[0][t]=0;
  // compute bottom-up as described
  return dp[n][0];
}
```
